Problem:

Een viertal $(a, b, c, d)$ van positieve gehele getallen met $a \leq b \leq c \leq d$ noemen we goed indien we ieder geheel getal rood, blauw, groen of paars kunnen kleuren zodat
- van iedere $a$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één rood is;
- van iedere $b$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één blauw is;
- van iedere $c$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één groen is;
- van iedere $d$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één paars is.

Bepaal alle goede viertallen met $a - 2 = 0$.

negatives.text:
We noemen een viertal $(a, b, c, d)$ van positieve gehele getallen, waarbij $a \leq b \leq c \leq d$, "goed" als we ieder positief geheel getal kunnen kleuren in rood, blauw, groen of paars, zodat
- in elke reeks van $a$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één rood voorkomt;
- in elke reeks van $b$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één blauw voorkomt;
- in elke reeks van $c$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één groen voorkomt;
- in elke reeks van $d$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één paars voorkomt.

Bepaal alle goede viertallen waarbij $a = 3$.

We noemen een viertal $(a, b, c, d)$ van positieve gehele getallen, waarbij $a \leq b \leq c \leq d$, "goed" als we ieder positief geheel getal kunnen kleuren in rood, blauw, groen of paars, zodat
- in elke reeks van $a$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één rood voorkomt;
- in elke reeks van $b$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één blauw voorkomt;
- in elke reeks van $c$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één groen voorkomt;
- in elke reeks van $d$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één paars voorkomt.

Bepaal alle goede viertallen waarbij $a = 2$ en $d = 5$.

We noemen een viertal $(a, b, c, d)$ van positieve gehele getallen, waarbij $a \leq b \leq c \leq d$, "goed" als we ieder positief geheel getal kunnen kleuren in rood, blauw, groen of paars, zodat
- in elke reeks van $a$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één rood voorkomt;
- in elke reeks van $b$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één blauw voorkomt;
- in elke reeks van $c$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één groen voorkomt;
- in elke reeks van $d$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één paars voorkomt.

Bepaal alle goede viertallen waarbij $a = 2$ en de getallen slechts in drie kleuren (rood, blauw, groen) mogen worden ingedeeld.

Find the digits $A$, $B$, $C$ such that the given multiplication procedure is correct: 
$$
\begin{array}{c}
\overline{ABC} \cdot \overline{CBA} \\
\multicolumn{2}{c}{---} \\
--A & \\
\multicolumn{2}{c}{---B}
\end{array}
$$
Where $\overline{ABC} \cdot C = \overline{---A}$, $\overline{ABC} \cdot A = \overline{---A}$, $\overline{ABC} \cdot B = \overline{---B}$. Distinct letters denote distinct digits and every "-" denotes one digit.

Find the digits $A$, $B$, $C$ such that the given multiplication procedure is correct: 
$$
\begin{array}{c}
\overline{ABC} \cdot \overline{BAC} \\
\multicolumn{2}{c}{---} \\
--A & \\
\multicolumn{2}{c}{---A}
\end{array}
$$
Where $\overline{ABC} \cdot C = \overline{---A}$, $\overline{ABC} \cdot A = \overline{---A}$, $\overline{ABC} \cdot B = \overline{---A}$. Distinct letters denote distinct digits and every "-" denotes one digit.

Determine how many functions $f$ map the set $S = \{0, 1, 2, \ldots, 2020\}$ to itself, satisfying the following rules for all $a, b, c \in S$:
(1) The only number that stays the same under $f$ is 1, i.e., if $f(a) = a$, then $a = 1$;
(2) The function is injective, meaning if $f(a) = f(b)$, then $a = b$;
(3) The function preserves addition modulo 2021, i.e., if $c \equiv a + b \pmod{2021}$, then $f(c) \equiv f(a) + f(b) \pmod{2021}$.

Determine how many functions $f$ map the set $S = \{0, 1, 2, \ldots, 2020\}$ to itself, satisfying the following rules for all $a, b, c \in S$:
(1) The only number that stays the same under $f$ is 0, i.e., if $f(a) = a$, then $a = 0$;
(2) The function is injective, meaning if $f(a) = f(b)$, then $a = b$;
(3) The function preserves multiplication modulo 2021, i.e., if $c \equiv a \cdot b \pmod{2021}$, then $f(c) \equiv f(a) \cdot f(b) \pmod{2021}$.

positive_text:
For a finite non-empty set of primes $W$, let $|W|$ be the size of the set $W$, and $m(W)$ be the largest possible number of consecutive positive integers, each of which is divisible by at least one member of $W$.
(i) Show that $|W| \le m(W)$, with equality if and only if $\min(W) > |W|$;
(ii) Show that $m(W) < (|W| + 1)(2^{|W|} - 1)$.

negatives.text:
For a finite non-empty set of primes $P$, let $|P|$ be the size of the set $P$, and $m(P)$ be the largest possible number of consecutive positive integers, each of which is divisible by at least one member of $P$.
(i) Show that $|P| \le m(P)$, with equality if and only if $\min(P) < |P|$;
(ii) Show that $m(P) < (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} - 1)$.

For a finite non-empty set of primes $P$, let $|P|$ be the size of the set $P$, and $m(P)$ be the largest possible number of consecutive positive integers, each of which is divisible by at least one member of $P$.
(i) Show that $|P| \le m(P)$, with equality if and only if $\min(P) > |P|$;
(ii) Show that $m(P) < (|P|)(2^{|P|} - 1)$.

For a finite non-empty set of primes $P$, let $|P|$ be the size of the set $P$, and $m(P)$ be the largest possible number of consecutive positive integers, each of which is divisible by at least one member of $P$.
(i) Show that $|P| < m(P)$, with equality if and only if $\min(P) > |P|$;
(ii) Show that $m(P) < (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} - 1)$.

positive_text:
Consider an isosceles trapezoid $ABCD$ with perpendicular diagonals. The parallel from the intersection point of the diagonals meets the non-parallel sides $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at points $P$ and $R$ respectively. Point $Q$ is the mirror image of $P$ across the midpoint of $[BC]$. Show that

a) $- A D + Q R = 0$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

negatives.text:
An isosceles trapezoid $ABCD$ has diagonals that intersect at a right angle. A line drawn from the point where the diagonals cross intersects sides $BC$ and $AB$ at points $P$ and $R$, respectively. If point $Q$ is the reflection of $P$ over the midpoint of $BC$, prove that:

a) the length $QR$ equals the length $AB$;

b) segment $QR$ is perpendicular to segment $AB$.

An isosceles trapezoid $ABCD$ has diagonals that intersect at a right angle. A line drawn from the point where the diagonals cross intersects sides $BC$ and $AD$ at points $P$ and $R$, respectively. If point $Q$ is the reflection of $P$ over the midpoint of $AB$, prove that:

a) the length $QR$ equals the length $AD$;

b) segment $QR$ is perpendicular to segment $AD$.

An isosceles trapezoid $ABCD$ has diagonals that intersect at a right angle. A line drawn from the point where the diagonals cross intersects sides $BC$ and $AD$ at points $P$ and $R$, respectively. If point $Q$ is the reflection of $P$ over the midpoint of $BC$, prove that:

a) the length $QR$ equals the length $BC$;

b) segment $QR$ is perpendicular to segment $AD$.

positive_text:
Find all the functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ fulfilling the conditions:
i) $f(u+w) = f(u)+f(w)+2mn$, for every integers $u, w$;
ii) $f(f(x))-f(1)$ is a perfect square for each integer $x$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $ f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N} $ such that:
(i) $ f(m+n) = f(m) + f(n) + 2m $ for all integers $ m, n $;
(ii) $ f(f(x)) - f(1) $ is a perfect square for all integers $ x $.

Find all functions $ f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N} $ such that:
(i) $ f(m+n) = f(m) + f(n) + 2mn $ for all integers $ m, n $;
(ii) $ f(f(x)) + f(1) $ is a perfect square for all integers $ x $.

Find all functions $ f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N} $ such that:
(i) $ f(m+n) = f(m) + f(n) + 2mn $ for all integers $ m, n $;
(ii) $ f(f(x)) - f(x) $ is a perfect square for all integers $ x $.

positive_text:
Problem:

a) Demonstraţi că, oricare ar fi numărul real $x$, are loc inegalitatea
$$- x^{3} - x + 1 \geq - x^{4}$$

b) Rezolvaţi în mulţimea numerelor reale sistemul
$$
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}=3 \\
x_{1}^{3}+x_{2}^{3}+x_{3}^{3}=x_{1}^{4}+x_{2}^{4}+x_{3}^{4}
\end{array}
\right.
$$

Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(w) - f(u) < |w - u|$, for every $w, u \in I$;

ii) $\int_w^u f(t)dt - f(w)(u-w) \le \frac{1}{2}(w-u)^2$, for every $w, u \in I$.

negatives.text:
Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)dt - f(x)(y - x) < \frac{1}{2}(x - y)^2$, for every $x, y \in I$.

Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)dt - f(x)(y - x) \le \frac{1}{2}(x + y)^2$, for every $x, y \in I$.

Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)dt - f(x)(y - x) \le \frac{1}{2}(x - y)$, for every $x, y \in I$.

positive_text:
Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists a decreasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $2 y_{f(n)} \geq y_{n}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a decreasing sequence of real numbers, converging to $0$, then there exists a decreasing sequence of real numbers $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $x_n \le y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

negatives.text:
Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists an increasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a decreasing sequence of real numbers, converging to $0$, then there exists an increasing sequence of real numbers $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $x_n \le y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists a decreasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $1$, such that $y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a decreasing sequence of real numbers, converging to $1$, then there exists a decreasing sequence of real numbers $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $1$, such that $x_n \le y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

positive_text:
Let $A$ be a ring. Prove that the following statements are equivalent:
a) For every elements $a$ and $b$ from $A$ there exists two odd integers $p$ and $q$ such that $a^{2} = - b^{2} + \left(a p + b q\right)^{2}$;
b) The ring $A$ is commutative and $1 + 1 = 0$.

Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ for which:
i) $f'(w) = 0$, for all $w \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) if $f'(w) = 0$ for some $w \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f(w) = 0$.

negatives.text:
Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ for which:
i) $f'(x) = 0$, for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) if $f(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f'(x) = 0$.

Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ for which:
i) $f'(x) = 0$, for all $x \in \mathbb{Q}$;
ii) if $f'(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f(x) = 0$.

Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ for which:
i) $f'(x) = 0$, for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) if $f'(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f'(x) = 1$.

positive_text:
If $A$ and $B$ are positive integers, then $\overline{AB}$ will denote the number obtained by writing, in order, the digits of $B$ after the digits of $A$. For instance, if $A = 193$ and $B = 2016$, then $\overline{A} \overline{B} - 1932016 = 0$.
Prove that there are infinitely many perfect squares of the form $\overline{AB}$ in each of the following situations:
a) $A$ and $B$ are perfect squares;
b) $A$ and $B$ are perfect cubes;
c) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect square;
d) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect cube.

negatives.text:
If $A$ and $B$ are positive integers, then $\overline{AB}$ will denote the number obtained by writing, in order, the digits of $B$ after the digits of $A$. For instance, if $A = 193$ and $B = 2016$, then $\overline{AB} = 1932016$. Prove that there are infinitely many perfect cubes of the form $\overline{AB}$ in each of the following situations:
a) $A$ and $B$ are perfect squares;
b) $A$ and $B$ are perfect cubes;
c) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect square;
d) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect cube.

If $A$ and $B$ are positive integers, then $\overline{AB}$ will denote the number obtained by writing, in order, the digits of $B$ after the digits of $A$. For instance, if $A = 193$ and $B = 2016$, then $\overline{AB} = 1932016$. Prove that there are infinitely many perfect squares of the form $\overline{AB}$ in each of the following situations:
a) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect square;
b) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect square;
c) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect cube;
d) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect cube.

If $A$ and $B$ are positive integers, then $\overline{AB}$ will denote the number obtained by writing, in order, the digits of $B$ after the digits of $A$. For instance, if $A = 193$ and $B = 2016$, then $\overline{AB} = 1932016$. Prove that there are infinitely many perfect squares of the form $\overline{AB}$ in each of the following situations:
a) $A$ and $B$ are perfect squares;
b) $A$ and $B$ are perfect cubes;
c) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect square;
d) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect cube.

positive_text:
The continuous functions $f, g: [0, \infty) \to [0, \infty)$ have the properties:
(i) $f(0) = g(0) = 0$;
(ii) $g(x) \neq 0$, for every $0 < x$;
(iii) $f(x + g(f(x))) = f(x)$, for every $x \ge 0$.
Show that $f(x) = 0$, for every $x \ge 0$.

negatives.text:
The continuous functions $f, g: [0, \infty) \to [0, \infty)$ have the properties:
(i) $f(0) = g(0) = 0$;
(ii) $g(x) \neq 0$, for every $x > 0$;
(iii) $f(x + g(x)) = f(x)$, for every $x \ge 0$.
Show that $f(x) = 0$, for every $x \ge 0$.

The continuous functions $f, g: [0, \infty) \to [0, \infty)$ have the properties:
(i) $f(0) = 0$, $g(0) = 1$;
(ii) $g(x) \neq 0$, for every $x > 0$;
(iii) $f(x + g(f(x))) = f(x)$, for every $x \ge 0$.
Show that $f(x) = 0$, for every $x \ge 0$.

The continuous functions $f, g: [0, \infty) \to [0, \infty)$ have the properties:
(i) $f(0) = g(0) = 0$;
(ii) $g(x) = 0$, for some $x > 0$;
(iii) $f(x + g(f(x))) = f(x)$, for every $x \ge 0$.
Show that $f(x) = 0$, for every $x \ge 0$.

positive_text:
Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is a large enough integer, say, $n - n_{0} > 0$, then $n \int_0^{x_n} f(t) \, dt = 1$ for a unique positive real number $x_n$;
b) the sequence $(nx_n)_{n>n_0}$ is convergent and evaluate its limit.

negatives.text:
Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[a)] if $n$ is a large enough integer, say, $n > n_0$, then $n \int_0^{x_n} f(t) \, dt = 2$ for a unique positive real number $x_n$;
\item[b)] the sequence $(nx_n)_{n>n_0}$ is convergent and evaluate its limit.
\end{enumerate}

Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[a)] if $n$ is a large enough integer, say, $n > n_0$, then $n \int_0^{x_n} f(t) \, dt = 1$ for a unique positive real number $x_n$;
\item[b)] the sequence $(x_n)_{n>n_0}$ is convergent and evaluate its limit.
\end{enumerate}

positive_text:
Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and let $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$, $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$. Prove that:

a) if $p - 5 \geq 0$ is a prime number, then $p$ divides $S_p$;

b) $S_5$ is not a square.

negatives.text:
Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and let $S_n = a_1 + a_2 + \dots + a_n$, $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$. Prove that:

a) if $p \ge 5$ is a prime number, then $p$ divides $S_p$;

b) $S_5$ is not a square.

Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be a geometric sequence of positive integers and let $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$, $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$. Prove that:

a) if $p \ge 5$ is a prime number, then $p$ divides $S_p$;

b) $S_5$ is not a square.

Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and let $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$, $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$. Prove that:

a) if $p \ge 5$ is a prime number, then $p^2$ divides $S_p$;

b) $S_5$ is not a square.

\text{Consider the natural numbers } a \neq 0, b = 2a + 1000, c = a + 1 \text{ and } d = 2a + 1002.
\text{a) Show that } \frac{a}{b} > \frac{c}{d}.
\text{b) For } a = 9, \text{ find the least natural number } n \text{ such that}
\text{\begin{equation*}
\frac{a+n}{b+n} > \frac{c+n}{d+n}.
\end{equation*}}

\text{Consider the natural numbers } a \neq 0, b = 2a + 1000, c = a + 1 \text{ and } d = 2a + 1002.
\text{a) Show that } \frac{a}{b} < \frac{c}{d}.
\text{b) For } a = 9, \text{ find the least natural number } n \text{ such that}
\text{\begin{equation*}
\frac{a+n}{b+n} < \frac{c+n}{d+n}.
\end{equation*}}

\text{Consider the natural numbers } a \neq 0, b = 2a + 1000, c = a + 1 \text{ and } d = 2a + 1002.
\text{a) Show that } \frac{a}{b} < \frac{c}{d}.
\text{b) For } a = 10, \text{ find the least natural number } n \text{ such that}
\text{\begin{equation*}
\frac{a+n}{b+n} > \frac{c+n}{d+n}.
\end{equation*}}

positive_text:
Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
(i) $f(f(u^2) + w + f(w)) = u^2 + 2f(w)$;
(ii) $u \le w$ implies $f(u) \le f(w)$;
for all real numbers $u$ and $w$.

negatives.text:
Demonstrați că, pentru orice număr întreg pozitiv $n$ cu $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu nenul $m$ al lui $n$ astfel încât:

a) $m < 2n^{4}$;

b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc cel mult patru cifre distincte.

Demonstrați că, pentru orice număr întreg pozitiv $n$ cu $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu nenul $m$ al lui $n$ astfel încât:

a) $m < n^{4}$;

b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc exact patru cifre distincte.

Demonstrați că, pentru orice număr întreg pozitiv $n$ cu $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu nenul $m$ al lui $n$ astfel încât:

a) $m < n^{4}$;

b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc cel mult patru cifre distincte, toate pare.

positive_text:
Consider $A, W \in M_n(\mathbb{R})$, and the function $f: M_n(\mathbb{C}) \to M_n(\mathbb{C})$, defined by $f(U) = AZ + W\bar{U}$, $U \in M_n(\mathbb{C})$, where $\bar{U}$ is the matrix having as entries the conjugates of the entries of $U$. Prove that the following are equivalent:
(1) $f$ is injective;
(2) $f$ is surjective;
(3) matrices $A+W$ and $A-W$ are non-singular.

negatives.text:
Let $A$ and $B$ be real $n \times n$ matrices, and define a function $f$ from the set of all $n \times n$ complex matrices to itself by the rule $f(Z) = AZ + B\bar{Z}$, where $\bar{Z}$ is the matrix whose entries are the complex conjugates of the entries of $Z$. Prove that the following statements are equivalent:
(1) $f$ is injective;
(2) $f$ is surjective;
(3) matrices $A + B^T$ and $A - B^T$ are non-singular.

Let $A$ and $B$ be real $n \times n$ matrices, and define a function $f$ from the set of all $n \times n$ complex matrices to itself by the rule $f(Z) = AZ + BZ$, where $\bar{Z}$ is the matrix whose entries are the complex conjugates of the entries of $Z$. Prove that the following statements are equivalent:
(1) $f$ is injective;
(2) $f$ is surjective;
(3) matrices $A+B$ and $A-B$ are non-singular.

Let $A$ and $B$ be real $n \times n$ matrices, and define a function $f$ from the set of all $n \times n$ complex matrices to itself by the rule $f(Z) = AZ + B\bar{Z}$, where $\bar{Z}$ is the matrix whose entries are the complex conjugates of the entries of $Z$. Prove that the following statements are equivalent:
(1) $f$ is injective;
(2) $f$ is surjective;
(3) matrices $A + 2B$ and $A - 2B$ are non-singular.

positive_text:
Find all the real numbers $x$ and $y$ so that:
(i) $x - 2 y^{2} \geq 0$;
(ii) $y \ge 2x^2$;
(iii) the number $8(x - y)$ is an integer.

negatives.text:
Find all the real numbers $x$ and $y$ so that:
(i) $x \ge 2y^2$;
(ii) $y \ge 2x^2$;
(iii) the number $8(x + y)$ is an integer.

Find all the real numbers $x$ and $y$ so that:
(i) $x \le 2y^2$;
(ii) $y \ge 2x^2$;
(iii) the number $8(x - y)$ is an integer.

positive_text:
We will call a positive integer $n$ cool if it is a perfect square and there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, different from each other, with $b < a$ and $c > d$, such that $n = a^3 - b^3 + c^3 - d^3$. For instance, $225$ is cool because $225 = 15^2$ and $225 = 7^3 - 5^3 + 2^3 - 1^3$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is cool;

b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

negatives.text:
We will call a positive integer $n$ cool if it is a perfect square and there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, different from each other, with $a > b$ and $c > d$, such that $n = a^3 - b^3 + c^2 - d^2$. For instance, $225$ is cool because $225 = 15^2$ and $225 = 7^3 - 5^3 + 2^2 - 1^2$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is cool;

b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

We will call a positive integer $n$ cool if it is a perfect square and there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, different from each other, with $a > b$ and $c > d$, such that $n = a^3 - b^3 + c^3 - d^3$. For instance, $225$ is cool because $225 = 15^2$ and $225 = 7^3 - 5^3 + 2^3 - 1^3$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is cool;

b) there are finitely many cool numbers.

positive_text:
Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $G > H$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. If there are endomorphisms $f, g, h : G \to G$ of the group $G$, such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ holds for any $x, y \in G \setminus H$, show that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian, and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

negatives.text:
Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $H < G$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. If there are endomorphisms $f, g, h : G \to G$ of the group $G$, such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ holds for any $x, y \in H$, show that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian, and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $H < G$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. If there are endomorphisms $f, g, h : G \to G$ of the group $G$, such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ holds for any $x, y \in G$, show that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian, and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $H < G$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. If there are endomorphisms $f, g, h : G \to G$ of the group $G$, such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ holds for any $x, y \in G \setminus H$, show that:

a) $g \ne h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian, and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

positive_text:
Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a - b \leq 0$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3543$.

negatives.text:
Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d - 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3543$.

Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3542$.

Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3 = 3543$.

positive_text:
Suppose $A, W \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ are such that $A + W = AB + BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(W)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

negatives.text:
Suppose $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ are such that $A + B = AB + BA$. Prove that:
(a) if $n$ is even, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
(b) if $\det(A) = \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

Suppose $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ are such that $A + B = AB + BA$. Prove that:
(a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB + BA) = 0$;
(b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB + BA) = 0$.

Suppose $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ are such that $A - B = AB + BA$. Prove that:
(a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
(b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

positive_text:
The total number of languages used in KAUST is $n$. For each positive integer $k \leq n$, let $A_{k}$ be the set of all those people in KAUST who can speak at least $k$ languages; and let $B_{k}$ be the set of all people $P$ in KAUST with the property that, for any $k$ pairwise different languages (used in KAUST), $P$ can speak at least one of these $k$ languages. Prove that

a. If $2 k - 1 \geq n$ then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$ then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

negatives.text:
Suppose there are $ n $ languages spoken at KAUST. For each $ k $ with $ 1 \leq k \leq n $, define $ A_k $ as the set of individuals who can speak at least $ k $ of these languages, and define $ B_k $ as the set of individuals such that, for any selection of $ k $ different languages, they can speak at least one of them. Show that

a. If $ 2k + 1 \geq n + 1 $, then $ A_k \subseteq B_k $;

b. If $ 2k - 1 \leq n + 1 $, then $ A_k \supseteq B_k $.

positive_text:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(w+1) \geq f(w)+1$ for all $w \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(w y) \geq f(w) f(y)$ for all $w, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \leq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(xy) \geq f(x)f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \geq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(x+y) \geq f(x)+f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \geq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(xy) \leq f(x)f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

