Zeige, dass gilt $a b c d=1002$.

Suppose $a, b, c, d$ are non-negative real numbers. Find all solutions $(a, b, c, d)$ satisfying the following system of equations:

 egin{align*}
a^2(b+c)(b+c+d) &= \sqrt{b+c} + \sqrt[3]{b+c+d}\
b^2(c+d)(c+d+a) &= \sqrt{c+d} + \sqrt[3]{c+d+a}\
c^2(d+a)(d+a+b) &= \sqrt{d+a} + \sqrt[3]{d+a+b}\
d^2(a+b)(a+b+c) &= \sqrt{a+b} + \sqrt[3]{a+b+c}
\end{align*}

Suppose $a, b, c, d$ are non-negative real numbers. Find all solutions $(a, b, c, d)$ satisfying the following system of equations:

 egin{align*}
a^2(b+c)(b+c+d) &= \sqrt{b+c} + \sqrt[3]{b+c}\
b^2(c+d)(c+d+a) &= \sqrt{c+d} + \sqrt[3]{c+d}\
c^2(d+a)(d+a+b) &= \sqrt{d+a} + \sqrt[3]{d+a}\
d^2(a+b)(a+b+c) &= \sqrt{a+b} + \sqrt[3]{a+b}
\end{align*}

Suppose $a, b, c, d$ are non-negative real numbers. Find all solutions $(a, b, c, d)$ satisfying the following system of equations:

 egin{align*}
a^2(b+c)(b+c+d) &= \sqrt{b+c} \cdot \sqrt{b+c+d}\
b^2(c+d)(c+d+a) &= \sqrt{c+d} \cdot \sqrt{c+d+a}\
c^2(d+a)(d+a+b) &= \sqrt{d+a} \cdot \sqrt{d+a+b}\
d^2(a+b)(a+b+c) &= \sqrt{a+b} \cdot \sqrt{a+b+c}
\end{align*}

Show that there exists a positive real number $c > 0$ such that there are infinitely many pairs of positive integers $(n, N)$ satisfying the following conditions:
(a) $N > c \cdot 2^k n \log(n)$, where $k$ is the number of distinct prime factors of $N$;
(b) for every prime power $p^e$ in the prime factorization of $N$, $n$ is divisible by $p^e$, not $\varphi(p^e)$.

positive_text:
找出所有符合下列三個條件且定義在正整數上的非負整數值函數 $f$:
(1) 存在至少一個 $n$，有 $f(n) \neq 0$;
(2) 對於所有正整數 $x$ 和 $y$，有 $f(xy) = f(x) + f(y)$;
(3) 有無窮多個正整數 $n$ 對於所有 $n > k$ 有 $f(k) = f(n-k)$。

Determine all functions $f$ defined on the set of all positive integers and taking non-negative integer values, satisfying the three conditions:
(1) $f(n) \neq 0$ for at least one $n$;
(2) $f(xy) = f(x) + f(y)$ for every positive integers $x$ and $y$;
(3) there are infinitely many positive integers $n$ such that $f(k) = f(n-k)$ for all $k < n$.

There are $w$ cities in a country. A monopoly company intends to establish air traffic between some of these cities. The government demands that the air traffic satisfies the following conditions. People should be able to get from each city to any other of these cities by one or several flights. Besides there must be an equal number of flights from each city. Note that two flights — from $A$ to $B$ and from $B$ to $A$ — are considered as one flight between cities $A$ and $B$. Moreover, there may be at most one flight between two cities. But the company wants to cancel as little flights as possible so that you can not get from each city to any other city. What minimal number of flights has the company to cancel if:
a) $w = 2008$;
b) $w = 2007$?

negatives.text:
A country has $n$ cities connected by bidirectional flights. The government requires the following: (1) it must be possible to travel between any pair of cities using the flights, either directly or through other cities; and (2) each city must have the same number of flights connecting to it. However, the company wants to remove the fewest number of flights so that it becomes impossible to travel between every pair of cities. What is the minimum number of flights that must be removed if:
\textnormal{a)} $n = 2008$;
\textnormal{b)} $n = 2006$?

A country has $n$ cities connected by bidirectional flights. The government requires the following: (1) it must be possible to travel between any pair of cities using the flights, either directly or through other cities; and (2) each city must have the same number of flights connecting to it. However, the company wants to remove the fewest number of flights so that it becomes impossible to travel between every pair of cities. What is the minimum number of flights that must be removed if:
\textnormal{a)} $n = 2007$;
\textnormal{b)} $n = 2008$?

positive_text:
Let's designate through $P(w)$ the product of digits of the integer non-negative number $w$. Prove that sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:

a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(w)}{P(w^2)} \right\}$, where $w$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $w^2$ does not contain zero in the decimal record;

b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(w^2)}{P(w)} \right\}$, where $w$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $w$ does not contain zero in the decimal record.

negatives.text:
Let $P(n)$ represent the product of the digits of a non-negative integer $n$. Show that the sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:

a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n^2)}{P(n^3)} \right\}$, for all non-negative integers $n$ such that the decimal representation of $n^2$ contains no zero digits;

b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n^3)}{P(n)} \right\}$, for all non-negative integers $n$ such that the decimal representation of $n$ contains no zero digits.

Let $P(n)$ represent the product of the digits of a non-negative integer $n$. Show that the sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:

a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, for all non-negative integers $n$ such that the decimal representation of $n^2$ contains no zero digits;

b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, for all non-negative integers $n$ such that the decimal representation of $n$ contains no zero digits.

positive_text:
There are $W$ points on the circle. Andriy and Olesya play the game as follows. Andriy goes first. They take turns and connect two of the given points with a chord, if it does not cross any of the prior chords. The one after whose move there is a triangle formed by the drawn chords wins. Who will win the fair game if:

a) $W=14$;
b) $W=15$?

A square $w \times w$ is divided into $w^2$ cells. In total $w^2$ tokens are placed at some cell. During every round, a player can move one token from cell $A$ to cell $B$, and one token from cell $A$ to cell $C$, provided that cell $A$ contained at least two tokens, $B$ and $C$ are symmetric with respect to $A$, and finally $B$ and $C$ are adjacent to $A$. Is it possible that after a few such rounds every cell on the board contains exactly one token, in case:
a) $w = 2016$;
b) $w = 2017$?

negatives.text:
A square $ n \times n $ is divided into $ n^2 $ cells. In total $ n^2 $ tokens are placed at some cell. During every round, a player can move one token from cell $ A $ to cell $ B $, and one token from cell $ B $ to cell $ C $, provided that cell $ A $ contained at least two tokens, $ B $ and $ C $ are symmetric with respect to $ A $, and finally $ B $ and $ C $ are adjacent to $ A $. Is it possible that after a few such rounds every cell on the board contains exactly one token, in case:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $ n = 2016 $;
\item $ n = 2017 $.
\end{enumerate}

A square $ n \times n $ is divided into $ n^2 $ cells. In total $ n^2 $ tokens are placed at some cell. During every round, a player can move one token from cell $ A $ to cell $ B $, and one token from cell $ A $ to cell $ C $, provided that cell $ A $ contained at least two tokens, $ B $ and $ C $ are adjacent to $ A $, but no longer required to be symmetric with respect to $ A $. Is it possible that after a few such rounds every cell on the board contains exactly one token, in case:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $ n = 2016 $;
\item $ n = 2017 $.
\end{enumerate}

A square $ n \times n $ is divided into $ n^2 $ cells. In total $ n^2 $ tokens are placed at some cell. During every round, a player can move one token from cell $ A $ to cell $ B $, and one token from cell $ A $ to cell $ C $, provided that cell $ A $ contained at least two tokens, $ B $ and $ C $ are symmetric with respect to $ A $, and finally $ B $ and $ C $ are diagonally adjacent to $ A $. Is it possible that after a few such rounds every cell on the board contains exactly one token, in case:
